= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Lie group> is a <smooth manifold> $G$ equipped with smooth maps $m:G\times G\to G$, $m(g,h)=gh$, and $\iota:G\to G$, $\iota(g)=g^{-1}$, for which multiplication is associative, there is an identity $e$, and every $g$ has the inverse $g^{-1}$. In equations,
$$
(gh)k=g(hk),\qquad eg=ge=g,\qquad gg^{-1}=g^{-1}g=e.
$$
Thus the <group axioms> and the smooth structure are compatible.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In a coordinate chart centred at the identity, smooth multiplication has a <local group law>
$$
z^k=F^k(y,x)=x^k+y^k+B^k{}_{ij}y^ix^j+O(3).
$$
The identity gives $F(0,x)=x$ and $F(y,0)=y$, while <associativity> gives the functional equation $F(u,F(y,x))=F(F(u,y),x)$. Smooth inversion determines coordinates $\bar x$ with $F(\bar x,x)=F(x,\bar x)=0$. Changes of local coordinates alter the symmetric part of $B^k{}_{ij}$, whereas its antisymmetric part supplies the coordinate-independent <Lie bracket> on the tangent space at the identity.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $g(x)=I+x^iT_i+O(x^2)$ in a <matrix representation> and $[T_i,T_j]=c^k{}_{ij}T_k$. Direct multiplication gives
$$
g(y)^{-1}g(x)^{-1}g(y)g(x)
=I+y^ix^j[T_i,T_j]+O(3),
$$
so
$$
w^k=c^k{}_{ij}y^ix^j+O(3)=-c^k{}_{ij}x^iy^j+O(3).
$$
The cancellation of the constant and linear terms explains why the <group commutator> first detects the <Lie bracket> at quadratic order.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The matrix is invertible precisely when $a\ne0$, and
$$
\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}^{-1}
=\begin{pmatrix}a^{-1}&-a^{-1}b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The largest group is therefore
$$
G=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}:a\in\mathbb R^*,\ b\in\mathbb R\right\},
$$
the <real affine group> $\mathbb R\rtimes\mathbb R^*$ under $(a,b)(a',b')=(aa',b+ab')$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

As a <smooth manifold>, $G\cong\mathbb R^*\times\mathbb R$. It has two <connected component>[connected components], distinguished by the sign of $a$: each of $a>0$ and $a<0$ is connected, and a continuous path in $G$ cannot cross $a=0$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The defining two-dimensional <group representation> is <reducible representation>[reducible], because the line $\mathbb Re_1$ is an <invariant subspace>:
$$
\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\0&1\end{pmatrix}e_1=ae_1.
$$
For $b\ne0$, the complementary line $\mathbb Re_2$ is not invariant, so this representation need not split as a <direct sum> of one-dimensional representations.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The map $(a,b)\mapsto\operatorname{sgn}(a)$ is a surjective <group homomorphism> with kernel $H_0$, so $H_0$ is a <normal subgroup> and $G/H_0\cong C_2$. The subgroup $H_1$ is not normal: for a translation $t=(1,c)$,
$$
t(a,0)t^{-1}=(a,c(1-a)),
$$
which leaves $H_1$ when $a\ne1$ and $c\ne0$. Finally, $(a,b)\mapsto a$ is a surjective homomorphism onto $\mathbb R^*$ with kernel $H_2$, so $H_2\triangleleft G$ and the <quotient group> $G/H_2\cong\mathbb R^*$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

